      "notes": "Existing HTTP owner, ordered repeated headers, body/redirect/cookie/screens. -s suppress progress (no routine progress output), -S keeps error, -sS bundle. -i/-D received status+headers CRLF; -w %{http_code},%{size_download},%{url_effective},%{time_total},%{time_namelookup},%{time_connect},%{time_appconnect},%{time_starttransfer},%{remote_ip},%{remote_port},%{content_type},%{http_version},%{num_redirects},%{redirect_url},%{size_upload},%{size_header},%{speed_download},%{exitcode},%% and escaped LF/tab, computed from the owner's observations of the exchange. -D - stdout; -o /dev/null typed sink. -b name=value or jar path, -c jar staged. URLencode variants name=value/name@path/@path/=value and multipart @path read through authority. -k/--insecure refuses with COMMAND_TLS_VERIFICATION_REQUIRED, also beside --cacert: the egress policy has no unverified TLS mode. --cacert FILE and --capath DIR are read through read authority and add trust for this request only; verification stays on. -E/--cert FILE[:PASS], --key FILE and --pass PASS read PEM client identity through read authority; a PKCS#8 encrypted key opens with the passphrase, which may be a managed secret reference and is never echoed. --cert-type accepts PEM only. -T FILE uploads exact bytes as the body (PUT unless -X), with the sent body identity on the receipt; -T - reads stdin; a URL without a file part gets the file name appended. --resolve HOST:PORT:ADDR and --connect-to HOST1:PORT1:HOST2:PORT2 change only where the hop connects: the override passes the same destination policy and loopback authority as a URL naming it, and the URL host stays for SNI, Host, cookies and secret-sink origin. -v writes * connection and TLS lines, > request line and headers as written and < response headers to stderr; Authorization and Proxy-Authorization show only their scheme. --fail-with-body exits 22 for status>=400 and keeps the body. -r RANGE sends Range: bytes=RANGE and reports 206 or 416 as received. --compressed requests deflate, gzip and decodes the body; -i/-D keep the received Content-Encoding. -A sets User-Agent. -G sends data parts as the query of a GET. --json sends concatenated JSON with application/json Content-Type and Accept unless set. -f is --fail. -x/--proxy, --noproxy and --retry stay unsupported: egress is mediated and there is no automatic HTTP retry or replay; non-idempotent unknown stays unknown. Loopback requires actual owned live listener or explicit current authority.",
